            <ArticleTitle>Plasma sCD28, sCTLA-4 levels in neuromyelitis optica and multiple sclerosis during relapse.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>52-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jneuroim.2011.11.010</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Soluble forms of CD28 (sCD28) and CTLA-4 (sCTLA-4) were associated with many autoimmune diseases like Sjögren's syndrome, systemic lupus erythematosus, asthma, and autoimmune myasthenia gravis. However, sCD28 and sCTLA-4 in neuromyelitis optica (NMO) and multiple sclerosis (MS) patients were less studied.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To measure the plasma sCD28, sCTLA-4 in NMO and MS patients, and investigate whether sCD28 and sCTLA-4 possible use as sensitive biomarkers for diseases activity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Plasma concentrations of sCD28, sCTLA-4 were measured by an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) in NMO (n=22), MS (n=21) patients and controls (n=18).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The concentration of sCD28 levels were higher in the inflammatory demyelinating diseases cohort compared with the controls (NMO, p=0.034; MS, p=0.026) and the levels of sCD28 were slightly higher in NMO compared with MS. The sCTLA-4 levels were lower in the MS subgroup compared with the controls (p=0.032). Both sCD28 and sCTLA-4 did not show any correlation with EDSS score in NMO and MS patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our study revealed for the first time that the levels of increased plasma sCD28 and decreased plasma sCTLA-4 in NMO and MS patients, but had little correlation with clinical presentations.</AbstractText>
